note When the graph's camera moves, and why it otherwise does not. Pan and zoom are two plain fields on GraphState, and like every graph field they change only through a GraphAction applied by GraphState::apply: the wheel sends Camera, dragging the background sends Pan, and the camera's own moves arrive as Look, Keep and Fitted. Nothing else writes them, so a rebuild, an edit or a re-index never slides the view out from under the reader. Two things ask for a move, each by setting a Wish to Wanted: a new focus (look: centre the node, or align its top when it is taller than the canvas) and the fit button (fit: the largest font at which the whole tree fits, then pan to its top-left; 1:1 resets the zoom and asks for a look). A node button that changes the tree's shape (callees, callers, more/less) instead keeps its node still: the pan follows the relayout so the button stays under the pointer and the new nodes appear beside it. The layout is in cells and rows, which no zoom changes, so fit settles both zoom and pan in one frame instead of converging over several. Both keep GRAPH_MARGIN pixels between the canvas edge and what they bring into view, and neither runs until the canvas element has a rectangle from last frame.

